    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. so you're saying it's bloom? i was confused bc i thought bloom meant something was growing or something lol. what exactly is happening with the fats at a molecular level to make that bloom appear on the surface? like why does it separate like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Bloom happens because the cocoa butter, which is the fat in the chocolate, starts to crystallize when exposed to fluctuating temperatures. When the fat separates from the rest of the chocolate matrix, tiny, dull-colored crystals form on the surface. These fat crystals are not chemically separate components; rather, they are the cocoa butter molecules re-arranging into a less stable, visible crystalline structure. Proper tempering manages this molecular alignment so only the desired, shiny crystals form.",
      "source": "target",
