      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, bloom is a strong indicator that the tempering process did not successfully guide the cocoa butter into its desired stable state. For dark chocolate, a common tempering curve involves melting to around 115-120°F (46-49°C), then cooling rapidly to the seeding temperature (around 82°F/28°C), and finally reheating slowly to the working temperature (about 88-90°F/31-32°C). Following this precise temperature sequence encourages the formation of the stable V-form crystals necessary for a glossy finish.",
      "source": "target",
